{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Spices for the anointing oil and for the incense of spices</b>. Some say this is an elliptical verse: \"spices for the anointing oil and spices for the incense of spices.\" And others said (Ibn Ezra in the name of the sages of the generation) that it is as though inverted, and for the incense they would bring spices. And he mentioned them with the definite article to say \"the good spices for burning,\" since there too he did not specify them. But R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (commentary on Exodus 25:6) that it is as it sounds: spices for both of them, for the anointing oil and for the incense, for spices of spikenard and saffron and cinnamon enter into the incense, according to the words of our Sages (Keritot 6a). And there was no need to mention \"spices and spices for the incense,\" because he mentioned \"the incense of spices,\" just as he did not mention \"oil for the anointing oil.\" And this is the correct view.<br>Now all these explanations were necessary, because according to the masters of the language, סַמִּים (samim) are the remedies, such as frankincense and galbanum, while בְּשָׂמִים (besamim) are those things eaten that are called confections, which have a good fragrance imparted to them. But according to Rashi's opinion, סַמִּים is a general term for spices, and so is the intent of our Sages (ibid. 6b) in the midrash: \"eleven spices were stated to Moses at Sinai,\" and so Onkelos translates (Onkelos on Exodus 25:6) בֻּסְמִין (busmin) for both of them. And this is the correct view in this verse.<br>But because Scripture varied the language, it is possible to say that the choicest among the fragrant substances are called בְּשָׂמִים (besamim), and its meaning is \"the select and well-known among the סַמִּים (samim),\" as he said, \"the chief spice\" (Exodus 30:23), \"at the head of every spice\" (Ezekiel 27:22). It is also correct to say that the word בְּשָׂמִים and בֹּשֶׂם (bosem) is a compound word: בּוֹ שָׂם (bo sam) — in it he placed סַמִּים (samim). And the proof for their words is that he said (Exodus 30:23) \"the chief spice of pure myrrh,\" and myrrh among the סַמִּים (samim) is considered a remedy, not a confection, and Scripture said (Song of Songs 4:14) \"nard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense,\" and it is written (ibid.) \"myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices,\" and concerning all of them he said (ibid. v. 16) \"Blow upon my garden, let its spices flow.\" Furthermore, Scripture said (Exodus 35:28) \"and the spice and the oil for the light, and for the anointing oil, and for the incense of spices,\" and he did not mention that they brought סַמִּים (samim) — if so, all of them are included in the בֹּשֶׂם (bosem)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:16:11.700622846Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}